<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saud ibn Abd al-Aziz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1748–1814</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Saud bin Abdulaziz, Saud the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saud ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a>, the first Saudi state, from 1803 to 1814. As commander and then ruler he led the state to its greatest extent: his forces sacked Karbala in 1802, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1803 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> soon after, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. His conquest of the holy cities provoked the Ottoman-Egyptian campaigns that destroyed the state after his death.</p></div>
